1.explanation:
All of the SQL syntax rules are defined inside the anonymous default export function in src/languages/sql.js. Within it there’s a string mode rule:
  { className: 'string', begin: '\'', end: '\'', contains: [{ begin: '\'\'' }] }
This rule only recognizes two single quotes ('') as an escape sequence, but when you have N''<text>;'' (escape at the very end), it doesn’t properly detect and close the string. As a result the highlighter never sees the final quote pair as a valid escape/context and everything afterward is colored as if it were still inside the string. To fix it, the string mode here needs to be extended (or its regex tweaked) to properly handle T-SQL’s N''…'' escape style even when it occurs right before the string terminator.
